\begin{abstract}
AI-assisted coding environments operate within finite context windows of 128,000 to 1,000,000 tokens (as of early 2026). Although providers continue to expand these limits, the core problem remains: when developers open many files, model attention becomes diluted and API costs increase quadratically with conversation length, yet developers lack tools to monitor or control their token consumption.  Tokalator is an open-source context engineering toolkit with four components: (1) a VS Code extension that monitors token budgets in real time, scores open tabs by syntactic relevance across five signals, closes distractor tabs on demand, and provides an interactive chat participant with 11 commands; (2) a web platform with seven interactive calculators based on CobbâDouglas quality modeling, caching break-even analysis ($n^* = 2$ for Anthropic models), and three conversation cost strategies with formal $O(T^2)$ cost proofs; (3) a reusable catalog of \hl{over 146 agents, 140 prompts, and 161 instruction files}; %% EMPTY FOLDERS: copilot-contribution/ has 1 agent, 3 prompts, 1 instruction; user-content/ is empty (.gitkeep only). Numbers are aspirational, not current. and (4) a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server and CLI that brings real Claude BPE token counting to Claude Code via stdio transport. %% UPDATE [v3.1.2]: "15 models" is wrong â modelProfiles.ts has 14 (4+7+3). Fix to "14 models" here and everywhere in the paper (abstract, highlights, comparison table, conclusions).
The toolkit covers \hl{14 models} from three providers (Anthropic, OpenAI, Google), is validated by 124 unit tests, and was introduced to over 220 developers through three community sessions and the VS Code Marketplace (\hl{77.62\% conversion rate, 163 acquisitions as of March~8, 2026}). %% RISKY CLAIM: "Most study participants... changing their tab management habits" â
