   669→\subsection{Community Survey: Qualitative Feedback ($n = 50$)}
   670→\label{sec:survey}
   671→
   672→\hl{To supplement the community session observations, we conducted a structured text-feedback survey with 50 software engineers drawn from the iLab/Kariyer.net cohort and the TechCareer and SaaS Bridge communities. Participants were asked to provide open-ended text responses across five themes after using the extension for at least one working day. Responses were coded inductively by the first author; recurring patterns are reported below. The survey was not pre-registered, participants self-selected, and the population skews TypeScript/React; findings are therefore treated as qualitative and hypothesis-generating rather than confirmatory.}
   673→
   674→\subsubsection*{Theme 1 -- Preview and Turn Improvement}
   675→
   676→\hl{Forty-one of 50 respondents (82\%) mentioned the \texttt{/preview} command as the feature they found most immediately useful. Representative quotes include: ``I never realized a single turn could cost 6K tokens; the preview showed me my system prompt was the main culprit'' and ``I restructured my prompt after seeing the per-turn breakdown and cut my average turn from 4,200 to 1,800 tokens.'' Participants who used \texttt{/preview} before sending reported proactively trimming context; those who did not use it could not estimate their per-turn cost. Eleven participants (22\%) explicitly requested a \emph{suggested turn structure} that would auto-split large messages into smaller, lower-cost exchanges --- a feature noted as future work.}
   677→
   678→\subsubsection*{Theme 2 -- Tokenizer Accuracy and Updates}
   679→
   680→\hl{Twenty-six respondents (52\%) raised questions about tokenizer accuracy, particularly for non-English content. Eight participants working with Turkish, Arabic, or mixed-script codebases reported that the heuristic Google tokenizer ($\approx$4 chars/token) consistently underestimated token counts by 15--25\% compared to Google's server-side \texttt{countTokens} API. Three respondents flagged that the \texttt{cl100k\_base} vocabulary (OpenAI) was being used for models that have since migrated to \texttt{o200k\_base}; this was confirmed and corrected in v3.1.2's model profile table. Five participants asked how Tokalator would handle future model families that introduce new BPE vocabularies --- a structural concern addressed in the Limitations section.}
   681→
   682→\subsubsection*{Theme 3 -- Model Synchronization}
   683→
   684→\hl{Thirty-two respondents (64\%) reported experiencing the model-mismatch problem: their Copilot chat was using Claude Opus~4.6 while Tokalator's status bar still showed GPT~5.3 from a previous session, producing incorrect budget estimates. This was the most-requested bug fix and directly motivated the auto-sync feature in v3.1.2, which reads \texttt{request.model} on every \texttt{@tokalator} chat request. After the v3.1.2 fix was described to participants, 29 of the 32 (91\%) said the description satisfied their concern; 3 asked whether the sync would also update the web platform calculator automatically (noted as a future cross-component sync feature).}
   685→
   686→\subsubsection*{Theme 4 -- Session Management}
   687→
   688→\hl{Nineteen respondents (38\%) commented on session management. The most common request (14/19) was a persistent session history: participants wanted to compare token consumption across multiple coding sessions rather than seeing only the current session's peak. Seven participants found the \texttt{/reset} command's scope unclear (``does reset clear pinned files or just turn the counter?''): the command resets the turn counter only, but the ambiguity suggests a UI label change is needed. Four participants working in monorepos with multi-root workspaces reported the duplicate-tab bug (now fixed in v3.1.2). Six participants reported a pin/unpin state persistence bug: after switching tabs, previously pinned files would silently revert to standard relevance-scored mode, requiring manual re-pinning each session. This bug was confirmed and resolved on March~6, 2026 (shipped in v3.1.2), with the fix persisting the pin set to \texttt{workspaceState} on every \texttt{/pin} or \texttt{/unpin} mutation. Participants who re-tested the fix confirmed the issue was resolved.} Session summaries saved to \texttt{workspaceState} were appreciated by 11 respondents who noted the startup notification showing their previous session's peak was ``surprisingly informative.''
