\hl{These figures demonstrate that AI-assisted development at the individual practitioner level is economically accessible: a commercially publishable, 20,814-LOC multi-component toolkit was constructed in 30 calendar days for a total net AI compute cost of \$28.36. Context-aware tooling --- the very capability Tokalator provides --- directly enables this cost efficiency: by monitoring token budgets and closing low-relevance tabs before sending requests, developers reduce both the token count per request and the number of requests needed to achieve a correct result. The 49.8\% effective discount observed here reflects GitHub's account-level billing credits rather than in-session optimisation;} instrumented studies comparing token-budget-aware vs.\ unaware workflows remain a priority for future work (Section~\ref{sec:future}).

\subsection{Broader Impact}
Some broader impacts of the toolkit are as follows:
\textit{(i) Cost optimization without the math.} The web platform transforms economic theory into interactive tools accessible to developers without a background in LLM economics. Break-even analysis, Cobb–Douglas optimization, and conversation cost projection are each reduced to a form entry and a chart.

\textit{(ii) Educational resources.} The 10-lesson context engineering course at \url{https://tokalator.wiki/learn} progresses from token basics to production compaction patterns. The automated wiki aggregates articles from arXiv, OpenAI Cookbook, Anthropic, and Google AI docs on a bi-monthly schedule. The dictionary defines 41 terms across seven categories, contributing to vocabulary standardization in a field where terminology is still inconsistent~\cite{burnham2025context}.

\textit{(iii) Ecosystem contribution.} \hl{The catalog of 146+ agents, 140+ prompts, and 161+ instruction files, auto-discovered from \texttt{copilot-contribution/} and \texttt{user-content/},} provides a structured starting point for teams adopting context engineering practices. %% EMPTY FOLDERS: actual counts are 1 agent, 3 prompts, 1 instruction; user-content/ is empty. The MCP server enables any Claude Code user to access real BPE token counts without an API key or network call. \hl{Tokalator's context management best practices are also published as a reusable agent skill on skills.sh, installable via \texttt{npx skills add vfaraji89/tokalator} (\url{https://skills.sh}), making the token budget workflow available to any agent that supports the Agent Skills specification.} %% UNDER REVIEW: SKILL.md exists at skills/context-management/SKILL.md but has not been approved on skills.sh yet; npx command will fail until review clears.
%% UPDATE [v3.1.2]: Add the skills.sh skill here. Suggested addition:
%%   "Tokalator's context management best practices are also published as a reusable agent
%%    skill on skills.sh (\url{https://skills.sh}), installable via
%%    \texttt{npx skills add vfaraji89/tokalator}, making the token budget workflow
%%    available to any agent that supports the Agent Skills specification."

\textit{(iv) Marketplace adoption.} %% UPDATE [v3.1.2]: Update this paragraph to reflect v3.1.2 release (tag pushed March 7, 2026).
%% The 156/188 figures are from the first 30 days of v3.1.x. Consider adding:
%%   "v3.1.2, released March 7, 2026, resolves the three bugs reported during
%%    community sessions and adds automatic Copilot model synchronization."
The VS~Code extension was published on the Visual Studio Code Marketplace on February~4, 2026. \hl{v3.1.2, released March~7, 2026, resolves the three bugs reported during community sessions and adds automatic Copilot model synchronization.} Within its first 30 days (February~4 – March~8, 2026), it recorded \hl{163~total acquisitions} (installs and downloads combined) from \hl{210~page views},
\hl{yielding a 77.62\% page-to-acquisition conversion rate (no published benchmark for the Marketplace average was available at time of writing).} \hl{Of the 163 acquisitions, 19 are organic direct installs initiated from within VS~Code or via the Marketplace install button — developers who discovered the extension independently, without referral links or community promotion.} Figure~\ref{fig:marketplace} shows the daily acquisition trend and conversion funnel: activity peaked around February~8--14, coinciding with the most intensive development period (46~commits on February~8) when the extension was promoted across internal and community channels. The sustained long-tail acquisitions through late February and early March reflect ongoing organic discovery via the Marketplace search. Combined with the 220+ developers reached through community sessions, these figures indicate genuine practitioner demand for context budget tooling.

