\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

The toolkit has several limitations worth noting.

\textit{(i) Approximate tokenization for Google models:} The extension uses a heuristic of approximately 4 characters per token for Gemini models, as Google does not provide a client-side BPE tokenizer for JavaScript. While Anthropic and OpenAI models use actual BPE tokenizers (\texttt{claude-tokenizer} and \texttt{o200k\_base}, respectively), the Gemini heuristic is based on the commonly observed ratio for English-heavy code corpora, where BPE tokens average 3.5 to 4.5 characters. We estimate $\pm$10–15\% error based on informal comparison with Google's server-side \texttt{countTokens} API on a sample of TypeScript, Python, and Markdown files. Languages with non-Latin scripts or heavily minified code may show larger deviations. This limitation is documented in the extension's dashboard through the tokenizer label display.

\textit{(ii) Extension performance overhead:} The extension subscribes to six VS Code event streams: active editor, tab changes, document edits, document open and close, diagnostics, and configuration changes. To prevent UI blocking, all event handlers use a 300 ms debounce timer, so at most one \texttt{refresh()} call occurs per 300 ms burst. Token counts are cached per document, keyed by URI and version number; re-tokenization occurs only when the document content changes. The BPE tokenizers are lazy-loaded (Claude ranks: 696\, KB JSON, o200k\_base: loaded via \texttt{js-tiktoken}), adding a one-time latency of approximately 100–200 ms on first use. A periodic background refresh (default 2 s, configurable) updates recency-based scores. In informal testing with over 30 open tabs, %% RISKY CLAIM: "<5 ms per snapshot" is from "informal testing" with one machine/config.
%% No methodology, hardware spec, OS, or statistical measure (mean/p95) reported. Reviewer
%% may ask for a reproducible benchmark. Also "imperceptible" is an uncited UX claim
%% (perceptibility threshold is typically cited as ~100ms in HCI literature).
the extension contributes less than 5 ms per snapshot build after the initial tokenizer warm-up, which is imperceptible to the user.

\textit{(iii) Syntactic rather than semantic relevance:}
The tab relevance scorer (Equation~2) uses structural signals --- import graphs, path similarity, language match, edit recency, and diagnostics --- not semantic understanding. A file that is conceptually important (e.g., a design document referenced in comments) will score low if it has no import relationship to the active file. Embedding-based similarity would improve accuracy but would add latency and a model dependency.

\textit{(iv) Estimated vs.\ actual context consumption:} The extension estimates how many tokens an AI coding assistant might include from open tabs, but the actual context construction logic of each assistant (GitHub Copilot, Cursor, Claude Code) is proprietary. The real context may include additional metadata (such as git diffs or terminal output) or exclude files the extension counts as open. Therefore, the estimates should be understood as upper-bound approximations.

%% LEXICAL: "remains an open empirical question" , academic filler. Simplified.
\textit{(v) No formal user study:} The toolkit has been introduced to 220+ developers across three community sessions (see Section~\ref{sec:impact}), but we have not run a controlled experiment measuring the effect of context budget visibility on code quality, task completion time, or developer productivity. The mathematical models (caching break-even, conversation cost projection) are validated through 124~unit tests against closed-form solutions (see Appendix~\ref{app:tests}), but the end-to-end productivity impact is not yet measured.


\textit{(vi) Pricing volatility:} LLM pricing changes frequently– Cottier et al.~\cite{cottier2025price} documented rapid but uneven price declines across tasks. The toolkit's pricing data is hardcoded in \texttt{lib/providers.ts} and requires manual updates. The modular design and CI pipeline make updates straightforward, but users should verify current pricing with provider documentation.

\textit{(vii) Single-IDE scope:} The extension currently targets only VS~Code and GitHub Copilot. While the web platform is IDE-agnostic, the real-time context monitoring functionality does not extend to JetBrains IDEs, Neovim, or other editors. The architecture (TypeScript, VS~Code Extension API) would require a separate implementation effort for each target platform.

\subsection*{Validity Threats}

\textit{(i) Internal validity.} The relevance score weights ($w_\mathrm{lang}=0.25$, $w_\mathrm{import}=0.30$, $w_\mathrm{path}=0.20$, $w_\mathrm{recency}=0.15$, $w_\mathrm{diag}=0.10$) were set by design reasoning, not measured against data. One source of bias is that the developer who chose the weights also observed the study participants, which may have skewed the evaluation. Future work should tune these weights against a held-out set of human relevance judgments.

\textit{(ii) Construct validity.} The extension estimates context consumption from open tabs, but the actual context construction of GitHub Copilot and Claude Code is proprietary. Consequently, the extension's token count is an upper-bound proxy, not a direct measurement. All reported figures (e.g., "21\% reduction") reflect estimated consumption, not confirmed API-level context size.

\textit{(iii) External validity.} The user study ran at three venues: Kariyer.net internal ($n \approx 50$), SaaS Bridge ($n \approx 90$), and TechCareer ($n \approx 80$), totalling $N > 220$ developers. The internal group shared a similar TypeScript/React stack; the community sessions had a wider mix of languages and roles. The results may not carry over to teams using compiled languages (C++, Rust), non-web domains, or different working styles. The sample is large enough to identify recurring feedback themes but not for statistical generalization.

\textit{(iv) Reliability.} The Cobb–Douglas sensitivity parameters ($\alpha$, $\beta$, $\gamma$) are placeholder values that satisfy structural constraints ($\alpha + \beta + \gamma < 1$) but are not calibrated from empirical data. Users who rely on the Economic Analysis tool for investment decisions should treat its outputs as directional rather than precise.



\section{Conclusions and Future Work}
\label{sec:conclusions}
\label{sec:future}


Tokalator is an open-source toolkit that surfaces and reduces the hidden token costs of AI-assisted development. This paper addressed three research questions. For \textbf{RQ1}, %% RISKY CLAIM: "20–30% of total context usage" in conclusions generalizes from a single
%% illustrative example (Example 1: 21.2%) and informal deployment observations.
%% This figure is not backed by a systematic study. A reviewer will note this.
%% Soften to "in our illustrative example, up to ~21%" or cite the deployment data explicitly.
we showed that instruction files and configuration tabs are the primary invisible budget consumers in typical coding sessions, \hl{contributing approximately 21\% of total context usage in our illustrative example}, and that real-time decomposition into five cost categories makes this immediately actionable. For \textbf{RQ2}, deployment and community presentations to over 220 developers across three venues (iLab/Kariyer.net, SaaS Bridge, TechCareer) showed that the five-signal relevance scorer flags low-relevance tabs (such as configuration files and unrelated language files) without false positives in our observational study. %% RISKY CLAIM: "All participants agreed" — same issue as in §4.2: universal claim from
%% informal, uncontrolled observation. No formal survey, no IRB, selection bias applies.
%% Soften to "Participants consistently agreed..." or report explicit counts.
\hl{Participants consistently agreed that the flagged distractors were appropriate candidates for removal.} For \textbf{RQ3}, closed-form economic models for caching break-even ($n^* = 2$ for Anthropic), conversation cost under three strategies ($O(T^2)$ vs.\ $O(T)$), and Cobb--Douglas quality optimization are validated by 124 unit tests and packaged into interactive calculators. Developers can answer questions like ``when does caching pay off?'' or ``how many turns can I afford?'' from a form and a chart, without working through the math themselves. %% UPDATE [v3.1.2]: Replace v3.1.1 → v3.1.2, and "15 models" → "14 models".
%% Also add: "v3.1.2 resolves the community-reported bugs (stale token counts on rapid
%% file switching, duplicate tabs in multi-root workspaces) and introduces automatic
%% model synchronization from the active Copilot chat model via request.model."
The current release (\hl{v3.1.2}) includes \hl{14 models} from three providers, a VS Code extension with 11 chat commands, a five-layer context monitoring architecture, and a Model Context Protocol server with CLI (\texttt{tokalator-mcp}) for Claude Code integration. \hl{v3.1.2 resolves the community-reported bugs (stale token counts on rapid file switching, duplicate tabs in multi-root workspaces) and introduces automatic model synchronization from the active Copilot chat model via \texttt{request.model}.} All components are validated by 124 tests across 6 test files and a CI pipeline.

Future work priorities, grounded in the survey findings (Appendix~\ref{app:survey}) and recent literature, are as follows.

\textbf{(1)~Controlled user study.} The qualitative survey ($n=50$, Appendix~\ref{app:survey}) identifies patterns but has no control group. A randomized experiment with before/after API cost measurements is needed to measure whether context budget visibility actually improves developer productivity and reduces spend. The Adaptive Context Management (ACM) framework of Perera et al.~\cite{perera2025acm} provides an evaluation baseline for conversational context optimization that could be adapted for this purpose.

\textbf{(2)~Cobb--Douglas calibration.} The sensitivity parameters ($\alpha$, $\beta$, $\gamma$) are currently author-assigned placeholders (Section~\ref{sec:functionalities}). Calibrating them requires experiments that vary token allocation while holding task difficulty fixed and measuring output quality. Fu et al.~\cite{fu2024scaling} provide a reproducible template: they systematically varied data mixture ratios and sequence lengths on code-generation benchmarks, a methodology that could be adapted here.

\textbf{(3)~Semantic relevance scoring.} The current five-signal scorer (Equation~2) misses files that are conceptually related but have no import links (Section~\ref{sec:limitations}). Two approaches from prior work could help: (a)~EVOR~\cite{shi2024evor}, which refines retrieved context documents iteratively during code synthesis, could be adapted to re-rank open tabs as the active task changes; (b)~position-aware token weighting~\cite{helm2025tokenweighting}, which shows that not all context positions contribute equally, supports scoring tabs by expected contribution rather than recency. Both would need embedding inference (e.g., a local ONNX model) to stay fast enough for the IDE.

\textbf{(4)~Compaction decomposition.} Survey Theme~5 (48\% of respondents) identified the inability to distinguish file-token growth from conversation-history growth as the primary gap in \texttt{/compaction} output. The ACM framework~\cite{perera2025acm} provides a modular design (Context Manager, Summarization, and Entity Extraction modules) that maps directly onto this decomposition: Tokalator could expose separate \textit{file-context} and \textit{conversation-history} growth curves in the \texttt{/compaction} report, each with its own projected compaction point.

\textbf{(5)~Multi-lingual tokenizer accuracy.} Survey Theme~2 revealed 15--32\% underestimation errors for Turkish and Arabic content under the 4-chars/token heuristic. Integrating Google's server-side \texttt{countTokens} API (with a fallback cache) would eliminate the heuristic for Gemini models. For multilingual coding teams, cross-lingual prompt optimization strategies~\cite{gupta2025crosslingual} (arXiv:2507.22923) suggest that token efficiency varies substantially with language and query strategy — a dimension Tokalator's calculator does not currently model.

\textbf{(6)~Session history dashboard.} Survey Theme~4 (38\% of respondents) requested persistent cross-session token history. Storing session summaries in VS~Code's \texttt{globalState} (rather than \texttt{workspaceState}) would allow cross-project comparison. A lightweight SQLite log could expose per-day peak usage, average turn cost, and model distribution in a new ``History'' tab in the sidebar dashboard.

\textbf{(7)~IDE support beyond VS~Code.} The architecture (TypeScript, VS~Code Extension API) requires a port effort for JetBrains, Neovim, and other editors. The \texttt{tokalator-mcp} CLI and MCP server already provide IDE-agnostic token counting; a Language Server Protocol (LSP) adapter could extend real-time budget monitoring to any LSP-capable editor without duplicating the core logic.

\textbf{(8)~Tokalator Pro: enterprise-grade context governance.} The community survey and marketplace adoption data indicate demand beyond individual developers. A planned \textit{Tokalator Pro} tier would add four enterprise capabilities: (a)~\textit{team dashboard} --- shared context budget visibility across an organisation, aggregating per-developer and per-project token consumption into a centralised view; (b)~\textit{historical analytics} --- persistent token usage logs queryable by day, sprint, model, and project, enabling spend forecasting and anomaly detection (complementing the session-history request from survey Theme~4); (c)~\textit{CI/CD budget gates} --- a configurable threshold check that fails pull-request builds when projected context consumption exceeds a policy limit, bringing context governance into the software delivery pipeline; and (d)~\textit{custom model profiles} --- a plugin mechanism for internal or fine-tuned models not in the default 14-model catalogue, with user-supplied BPE vocabulary files and pricing overrides. The open-source core remains MIT-licensed; Pro features would be delivered as a separately licensed server component with a local VS~Code panel.

The web platform at \url{https://tokalator.wiki} will continue to grow with community contributions to the catalog and automated wiki updates. All components are MIT-licensed and available at \url{https://github.com/vfaraji89/tokalator}.


