   656→\subsection{RQ3: Economic Models as Developer Tools}
   657→
   658→\textit{Mathematical validation.} All seven economic models are validated against closed-form solutions by 124 unit tests (Appendix~\ref{app:tests}). The caching break-even formula ($n^* = 2$ for all current Anthropic models) was independently verified: with $c_w = 1.25 \times c_\mathrm{in}$ and $c_r = 0.10 \times c_\mathrm{in}$, the formula yields $\lceil 1.25/0.90 \rceil = 2$, consistent with Anthropic's published pricing. The $O(T^2)$ conversation cost growth is proven analytically (Section~\ref{sec:functionalities}) and confirmed numerically in the Conversation Estimator tests.
   659→
   660→\textit{Usage evidence.} Example~2 (Section~\ref{sec:examples}) demonstrates that the Caching ROI Calculator produces a concrete, correct recommendation (88.9\% daily savings for a 50K-token system prompt with 100 daily reuses) from first principles without requiring users to understand the underlying formula. Example~3 shows that the Conversation Estimator correctly quantifies the cost difference between Full History (28 turns for a \$5 budget) and Sliding Window (83 turns), making the strategy choice actionable.
   661→
   662→\subsection{Community Survey: Qualitative Feedback ($n = 50$)}
   663→\label{sec:survey}
   664→
   665→We conducted a structured text-feedback survey with 50 software engineers from the iLab/Kariyer.net, TechCareer, and SaaS Bridge communities. Participants used the extension for at least one working day before responding. Responses were coded inductively; the survey was not pre-registered and participants self-selected, so findings are treated as qualitative and hypothesis-generating rather than confirmatory. Full theme descriptions, Likert distributions, demographics, and verbatim quotes are in Appendix~\ref{app:survey}.
   666→
   667→Five themes emerged. The \texttt{/preview} command was the most-valued feature (82\% of respondents), with participants discovering that a single turn can cost 6K+ tokens and adjusting their prompts after seeing the breakdown. Tokenizer accuracy was a concern for non-English users: developers writing Turkish or Arabic code found the Google heuristic ($\approx$4 chars/token) underestimated counts by 15--32\%. Model synchronization was the most-requested fix: 64\% of respondents noticed their Copilot chat model and Tokalator's status bar were out of sync, which v3.1.2 resolved by auto-reading \texttt{request.model} on every chat command. On session management, 38\% asked for persistent cross-session history, and several reported the pin/unpin bug now fixed in v3.1.2. Finally, 48\% of respondents used \texttt{/compaction}, with 67\% of that group saying it helped them spot the compaction point before hitting the context limit; the main gap was that the output could not yet separate file tokens from conversation tokens.
   668→
   669→\subsection{Real-World AI Development Cost Analysis}
   670→\label{sec:cost}
   671→
   672→\hl{To complement the qualitative and quantitative evaluations above, this section presents the actual GitHub Copilot billing data recorded during Tokalator's 30-day development sprint (February~6 -- March~7, 2026). The data provides a rare empirical trace of AI-assisted software engineering costs at the individual-developer level, covering 1,413 premium AI requests across three model families --- Claude Opus~4.6, GPT~5.3, and GPT Codex~5.4 --- used via the GitHub Copilot interface. All data were exported from the GitHub Copilot usage dashboard and reflect the} \texttt{copilot\_premium\_request} \hl{SKU at the published list price of \$0.04 per request.}
   673→
   674→Table~\ref{tab:billing-summary} \hl{summarises the key cost metrics. Total gross cost for the 1,413 premium requests was \$56.52. GitHub applied \$28.16 in billing credits (49.8\% effective discount), yielding a total net developer cost of \$28.36 --- less than \$30 for a full production-quality, 20,814-LOC, multi-component toolkit. GitHub Actions CI/CD consumed 261~minutes across 27 workflow runs (ci.yml, CodeQL security scanning, Dependabot updates, and wiki-fetch scheduling); all Actions usage fell within the free tier, contributing \$0 to net cost. The average net AI cost on active development days was \$1.49/day, peaking at \$8.20 on February~11 (the highest-cost session).}

