186:Hong, Troynikov, and Huber~\cite{hong2025contextrot} introduced the term \emph{context rot} in an evaluation of 18~LLMs, showing that model performance drops unevenly as input length grows, even on simple tasks like needle retrieval and word repetition. They kept task difficulty constant and varied only input length, finding that distractor content makes things worse and that a well-structured haystack does not help retrieval accuracy. These results back Tokalator's context health warnings, which alert developers when their context size passes a provider-specific rot threshold and flag low-relevance tabs as likely distractors. Anthropic's applied AI team took the same view in their context engineering guide~\cite{anthropic2025contexteng}, treating context as a limited resource with diminishing returns because of the transformer's $n^2$ attention cost. They described three strategies for long-running agents: \emph{compaction} (summarizing and restarting the context window), \emph{structured note-taking} (keeping critical state outside the window), and \emph{sub-agent architectures} (handing focused sub-tasks to agents with clean windows). These ideas directly shaped Tokalator's \texttt{/compaction} command.
578:We conducted a structured 10-item survey with 50 software engineers from the iLab/Kariyer.net, TechCareer, and SaaS Bridge communities. Data were collected in person during the three community sessions and in a follow-up online session for remote participants, using structured note-taking and consolidated into an Excel file; no third-party survey platform was used. Participants used the extension for at least one working day before responding. Responses were coded inductively; the survey was not pre-registered and participants self-selected, so findings are treated as qualitative and hypothesis-generating rather than confirmatory. Full theme descriptions, Likert distributions, demographics, and verbatim quotes are in Appendix~\ref{app:survey}.
