    61→\begin{abstract}
    62→AI coding assistants such as GitHub Copilot, Claude Code, and Cursor operate within finite context windows of 128,000 to 1,000,000 tokens. When developers accumulate open files, instruction files, and long conversation histories, model attention degrades and API costs grow quadratically with session length. Yet no cross-provider, cost-modeling tool has previously made these costs visible inside the IDE. We present Tokalator, an open-source intelligent developer assistance system that addresses this gap through four components: a VS~Code extension that monitors token budgets in real time, scores open tabs by syntactic relevance using five signals, and closes distractor tabs on demand via 11 chat commands; a web platform with seven interactive calculators implementing closed-form caching break-even analysis, Cobb--Douglas quality optimization, and three conversation cost strategies with proven $O(T^2)$ growth for full-history sessions; a context engineering catalog of reusable agents, prompts, and instruction files; and a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server and CLI for Claude Code integration. The system covers 14 models across three providers and is validated by 124 unit tests. A qualitative deployment study with 50 software engineers and community presentations to over 220 developers across three venues confirm that instruction files and low-relevance configuration tabs are the primary invisible budget consumers. In our illustrative example, Tokalator reduced context usage by 21.2\%; prompt caching analysis showed 88.9\% daily cost savings for a 50,000-token system prompt with 100 daily reuses. The VS~Code extension reached a 77.62\% page-to-acquisition conversion rate and 163 acquisitions within its first 30 days on the Marketplace.
